# CloudWatch Log Monitoring on EC2

## Simple Step-by-Step Guide

Follow each step in order and run the commands one by one.

---

## Step 1 — Create an IAM Role

Open:

**AWS Console → IAM → Roles → Create Role**

Select:

```text
Trusted entity: AWS service
Use case: EC2
```

Attach this permission:

```text
CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy
```

Create the role and attach it to your EC2 instance.

> This lets the CloudWatch Agent send logs and metrics without storing AWS Access Keys on the EC2 instance.

---

## Step 2 — Connect to EC2

Connect to the Ubuntu EC2 instance using SSH or EC2 Instance Connect.

---

## Step 3 — Update Ubuntu

Run:

```bash
sudo apt update -y
```

---

## Step 4 — Download the CloudWatch Agent

Run:

```bash
wget https://amazoncloudwatch-agent.s3.amazonaws.com/ubuntu/amd64/latest/amazon-cloudwatch-agent.deb
```

---

## Step 5 — Install the CloudWatch Agent

Run:

```bash
sudo dpkg -i -E ./amazon-cloudwatch-agent.deb
```

---

## Step 6 — Configure Log Monitoring

Start the configuration wizard:

```bash
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard
```

When the wizard asks which log file to monitor, you can use:

```text
/var/log/syslog
```

Example settings:

```text
Log Group Name: /ec2/syslog
Log Stream Name: {instance_id}
Initial Position: From start of file
```

Complete the wizard so the configuration file is created.

---

## Step 7 — Start the CloudWatch Agent

If the configuration file is here:

```text
/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/config.json
```

Run:

```bash
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl \
-a fetch-config \
-m ec2 \
-s \
-c file:/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/config.json
```

---

## Step 8 — Check Agent Status

Run:

```bash
sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl \
-a status \
-m ec2
```

The status should show:

```text
running
```

---

## Step 9 — Check Logs in AWS CloudWatch

Open:

**AWS Console → CloudWatch → Logs → Log groups**

Open the log group you created. You should see logs coming from the EC2 instance.

---

## Important

Do **not** use the old CloudWatch Logs Agent commands:

```bash
curl https://s3.amazonaws.com/aws-cloudwatch/downloads/latest/awslogs-agent-setup.py -O
```

```bash
sudo python3 ./awslogs-agent-setup.py
```

For a new setup, use the Amazon CloudWatch Agent shown above.

Do not upload AWS Access Keys, Secret Keys, passwords or credential files to GitHub. Use an IAM role attached to EC2.
